.SH NAME
machterm \- display server and terminal utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B machterm
[ \-sh | \-csh ]
.PP
.B machterm
\-status
[ <text> ]
.PP
.B machterm
\-invert
.PP
.B machterm
\-cursor
<cursor type>
.PP
.B machterm
<display device>...
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I machterm
program is a simple display server and terminal utility.
As a display server,
.I machterm
can handle multiple display devices and multiple virtual terminals
on each device.
As a utility program,
.I machterm
controls various terminal options, such as writing to the status
line, switching to inverse video and selecting cursors.
.SH OPERATION
The
.I machterm
display server uses the machterm library to present text on the
specified bit\-mapped displays.
Pseudo terminals are used to run the /bin/login program.
The terminal emulated by the machterm library supports a status
line, inverse video and multiple cursor types.
.PP
On a virtual terminal, the ^] key is used to enter commands
to the machterm server.  Use ^]\-? to display a list of the
commands.
.PP
The
.I machterm
program provides user access to these parameters.
The
.I machterm
program also provides the important function of setting up the
.I TERM
and
.I TERMCAP
environment variables for either /bin/sh or /bin/csh.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
machterm -sh
.PP
This queries the current machterm terminal for its termcap
specifications and generates code to setup the
.I TERM
and
.I TERMCAP
environment variables for /bin/sh.
Note that if this is NOT run under a machterm terminal,
it will have a non-zero exit code.
.IP
machterm -status "some text"
.PP
This write "some text" on the status line.
.IP
machterm -invert
.PP
This inverts the video display.
.IP
machterm -cursor close line
.PP
This changes the current cursor to be a close line.
If a cursor type is not specified, then a list of valid
cursor types is displayed.
.IP
machterm /dev/scn0 /dev/scn0 /dev/scn1
.PP
This runs two virtual terminals on display screen 0 and one
virtual terminal on display screen 1.
Note that the first virtual terminal on display screen 0 will
attempt to take over as the system console.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
The
.I machterm
program displays appropriate error messages.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
login(1),
machterm(3),
.SH BUGS
None so far.
.SH AUTHOR
The
.I machterm
program was written by Zonnie L. Williamson at CMU, 1992.

